--- !u!114 &1753929911
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1753929908}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 8d5be57ef61e14ff39321ed63a581266, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: AudioFeatureLab::AudioFeatureLab.DemoOverlay
  title: Input, output, and meters
  body: 'The music clip starts immediately so the bars have something to analyze.
    Space starts or stops the microphone. H switches the analyzer between that clip
    and the mic.

    N cycles input devices. K toggles monitor volume. Leave the
    monitor off unless you are on headphones, or the mic will howl.


    Select
    Microphone while playing. peak, peakDecibels, and rms are the public levels from
    the capture buffer.

    Select Band Analyzer. Each row in bands has a public
    level. Change minHz, maxHz, or boost, or add a row. The colored bars read those
    levels.

    Select Analysis Clip for OutputLevelMeter, which reads AudioSource.GetOutputData
    (the source after its own volume).


    The panel on the right lists Microphone.devices
    and the active output configuration: sample rate, DSP buffer, speaker mode, and
    voice counts. Unity does not offer a public list of output device names. The
    buttons call AudioSettings.Reset to change buffer size or speaker mode, which
    restarts audio.


    Menu is M, not Space, in this scene.


    M menu. P pauses
    every voice (AudioListener.pause). [ and ] change AudioListener.volume.

    Click
    the Game view so the editor does not take the keys.'
  scroll: {x: 0, y: 0}
  width: 480
